schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Funding Your Local Node
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: framework
  summary: >-
    A staged funding framework for local regen nodes: start free on purpose
    (free tools, learn real costs), move to member contributions and sweat
    equity, add earned revenue (workshops, goods, service fees) that stays
    local, manage money transparently via a fiscal host (Open Collective) or
    on-chain multisig (Safe) before scaling, then pursue external funding
    (Gitcoin Grants, Greenpill Network chapter rounds + hypercerts, ReFi
    DAO's Local Node program) only once the model is proven. Names concrete
    pitfalls (taking big money too early, over-funding, no Year-2 plan,
    letting the funding model reshape community values) and a realistic
    month-by-month sequence.
  audience: local regen node builders deciding how to fund the node
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      The "funding model shapes your community" table names real trade-offs
      (member fees risk excluding lower-income members; grants drift toward
      funder priorities; earned revenue pulls toward what sells; sweat
      equity risks burnout) without resolving them — presented as an honest
      choice, not a solved problem.
  related_concepts:
    - rosca-rotating-savings-and-credit-association
  related_resources:
    - safe
    - open-collective
    - gitcoin-grants
    - greenpill-network
    - refi-dao
    - giveth
    - hypercerts
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/funding-your-node.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded (wo-3a548f510339, funding-your-node.md):
# - "Try This" exercise prompts (answer five funding questions, set up a
#   transparent treasury, prepare a grant application draft) — reader action
#   items, not extractable knowledge-graph content.
# - The "funding model → community pull" comparison table (member
#   fees/grants/earned revenue/sweat equity) — folded into the encyclopedia
#   summary as a known tension rather than split into separate claim-evidence
#   objects; it is a set of design trade-offs, not individually sourced facts.
